orthant 1 release

-   def stage(out: Path on Linux): LocalClasspath = classpath(out)
+   def stage(out: Path on Local): LocalClasspath = classpath(out)

- Code which runs elsewhere can fail, and the failure has to come back. An error raised in the
- staged block is carried across and raised again at the call site. So `dispatch` reports the
- failure of the remote computation, and not that the remote computation could not be reached. A
- failure of the rig itself, such as a subprocess which would not start or a classloader which
- could not be built, is distinct, since the two call for different responses.
+ Code which runs elsewhere can fail, but an error raised in the staged block is not carried back
+ across the boundary as itself. `dispatch` raises only a `Compiler.Error`, if the staged code
+ does not compile, or a `Rig.Error`, for a failure of the rig. With the `Jvm` rig, staged code
+ which throws makes its subprocess exit with a failure, which is reported as a `Rig.Error` whose
+ reason is `Invocation`: the same error as a subprocess which would not start. Staged code which
+ may fail should therefore return its failure as a value.
